Package: oneplus-erhai-erhaicam
Version: 1.5
Section: graphics
Priority: optional
Architecture: arm64
Maintainer: TheMojoMan <themojoman@gmx.net>
Provides: oneplus-erhai-cam
Replaces: oneplus-erhai-cam (<< 1.3)
Breaks: oneplus-erhai-cam (<< 1.3)
Depends: libgtk-4-1, libgdk-pixbuf-2.0-0, gstreamer1.0-libcamera, gstreamer1.0-gtk4, gstreamer1.0-plugins-base, gstreamer1.0-plugins-good
Recommends: iio-sensor-proxy, gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad, python3-numpy
Description: ErhaiCam — standalone camera app for the OnePlus Pad 3 (erhai)
 GTK4/GStreamer camera app with NPU-driven autofocus and auto-exposure.  The
 per-frame image analysis (Tenengrad focus metric + luminance statistics) runs
 on the Hexagon NPU (HTP) as an ONNX graph executed by ONNX Runtime's QNN
 execution provider (erhai-npu-analyze.py), with a CPU implementation of the
 same metric as the automatic fallback.  The AF sweep drives the kernel
 cn3927 lens subdev (V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE, tap-to-focus, refocus on
 sharpness drop); AE is a log-domain controller on the SC1320CS integration
 time written straight to the sensor register 0x3e00.  AE (top left) and AF
 (top right) buttons toggle the automatics; turning one off reveals the
 matching vertical slider below the button for manual focus (VCM, 0..1023)
 or exposure (sensor lines, 1..6396).  Also: photo capture into
 ~/Pictures/ErhaiCam, video recording (v4l2h264enc/mp4mux with vp8/webm
 fallback), front/rear camera switching (front streams once its Phase-B
 driver work lands) and accelerometer orientation handling (upright preview
 + upright snapshots).
 Photo capture and autofocus are the focus; video is experimental.
 .
 This package takes over the renamed oneplus-erhai-cam (<= 1.2, the same app):
 it Provides that name, and Replaces/Breaks the old versions so an installed
 oneplus-erhai-cam upgrades here and its files (usr/bin/erhai-cam, the desktop
 file, the shipped source) change hands cleanly.  The oneplus-erhai-cam 1.3
 package is an empty transitional package that Depends on this one.
 .
 The manual-focus/exposure GNOME Shell extension (erhai-cam-focus@erhai.local)
 is retired: the app owns both loops natively now (oneplus-erhai-camera 1.3
 no longer ships it).
